stems consisting of distribution pipelines, backbone and local transmission pipelines, and various storage facilities. It serves residential, commercial, industrial, and agricultural customers, as well as natural gas-fired electric generation facilities. The company was incorporated in 1995 and is based in Oakland, California.","ceo_bio":"Ms. Patricia Kessler Poppe | CEO & Director | Age 56 | Compensation: $8.6M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 29K people. Revenue per employee of $860K ranks in the below the sector median among Utilities peers. Stock-based compensation of $5K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 10\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (8/10).","financial_summary":["Pacific Gas & Electric Co. is a large-cap utilities business with a $30.3B market cap on $24.9B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +5% over the past three years and +4% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 3% against a 6% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The company is burning through $3.1B of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 12.7\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.6\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 1.5%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 46% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.45954407257002466},{"text":"Piotroski score 